Thanks for the suggestion, but I would be -1 on modifying `to_numeric` in this way.

Although it's not "clean" just to do 

```
def parse_numbers(x):
    try:
        return pd.to_numeric(x)
    except Exception:
        return x

df[col].apply(parse_numbers)
```

This behavior was intentionally deprecated in pandas 2.x to avoid potentially returning `object` columns which don't have great support and poor performance characteristics.

> "coerce"+fillna would not retain object cols.

The full suggested snippet for that approach (as suggested [here](https://github.com/pandas-dev/pandas/issues/54467#issuecomment-2326438909)) was `df2 = df1.apply(pd.to_numeric, errors='coerce').fillna(df1)`.  Note that filling from the df was required to restore values such as the paths in the above example.  In my testing (v2.3.3) with a simple df defined as `df1 = pd.DataFrame(["1", "2", "3", ""])`, the dtype of the column was reverted when coerced + filled (though the values remained floats).  Because I don't wish valid numeric cols to get converted back, I personally implemented the hybrid below.  I recognize it is beyond the old behavior provided by "ignore", but it also better matches the original posts targeted behavior (imo).

```
def hybrid_parse(x):
    try:
        return pd.to_numeric(x)
    except Exception:
        return pd.to_numeric(x, errors="coerce").fillna(x)
```

With respect to: 

> `parse_numbers` is exactly the old "ignore" behavior

That was rather my point, while to_numeric may no longer be returning object types, I don't see how their use would be any less prevalent.  Anyone implementing the suggested replacements will be retaining object cols anyway.  I assumed that returning the unchanged values would really be no slower for to_numeric itself and that the broader context was a generic shift away from using object (due to the stated reasons).  It was with this in mind that I suggested an approach that might work to both ends (ie retain the flexibility once provided by "ignore" and still meet the design goals).

In the end, I've found it fairly common that imported data ends up needing conversion to numeric (ie from string).  When using pandas within ide's which display numeric data differently, the conversion of potentially numeric data has been particularly useful when evaluating datasets.  The "ignore" behavior assisted especially when reviewing new datasets or datasets whose full characteristics are not yet fully defined.

If you have a suggested API replacement I'm definitely interested, but so far, I haven't been able to find a better solution.

