@jorisvandenbossche -

I only checked `test_chained_assignment_deprecation.py::test_frame_setitem[a]`, but I would venture that the other failing tests have the same root cause. 

In this case the root cause is that `_PyObject_IsUniquelyReferenced`, a helper called by `PyUnstable_Object_IsUniqueReferencedTemporary`, returns a different result on each build if the object has weak references. 

 The default build only checks the refcount, so it treats objects with [weak references as unique](https://github.com/python/cpython/blob/a490d671fad24859a31afdb7a64f090deb3346c2/Include/internal/pycore_object.h#L186-L188):

```c
#if !defined(Py_GIL_DISABLED)
    return Py_REFCNT(ob) == 1;
#else
```

In contrast, the free-threaded build treats objects with [weak references as as non-unique](https://github.com/python/cpython/blob/a490d671fad24859a31afdb7a64f090deb3346c2/Include/internal/pycore_object.h#L189-L194):

```c
    // NOTE: the entire ob_ref_shared field must be zero, including flags, to
    // ensure that other threads cannot concurrently create new references to
    // this object.
    return (_Py_IsOwnedByCurrentThread(ob) &&
            _Py_atomic_load_uint32_relaxed(&ob->ob_ref_local) == 1 &&
            _Py_atomic_load_ssize_relaxed(&ob->ob_ref_shared) == 0);
```
(The low bit of `ob->ob_ref_shared` will be `1` if the object has weak references.)

It looks like pandas is creating weakrefs to the returned temporaries, causing the divergence in behavior that you see. I'm not sure which behavior is correct, but I'd lean towards being conservative and updating the default build to match the free-threaded behavior. Tagging @colesbury in case he has thoughts.

